    {
      "type": "image",
      "visible": true,
      "sectionLabel": "CORE COMPARISON",
      "title": "VLA, WAM, and GAM Paradigms",
      "content": {
        "media": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "",
            "caption": "GAM moves world modeling from 2D video space into 3D geometric latent space.",
            "alt": "Figure 1 from paper - side-by-side comparison of Video WAMs, geometry-aware VLAs, and GAM. Video WAMs predict future RGB latents and actions with video diffusion. Geometry-aware VLAs distill GFM features into a VLA. GAM inserts a causal future predictor inside a single GFM and decodes both future geometry and actions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "id": "091cfea7"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "visible": true,
      "sectionLabel": "POSITIONING",
      "title": "From VLA and WAM to GAM",
      "content": {
        "bulletItems": [
          "VLAs use strong language and semantic priors, but their action decoding largely depends on 2D visual representations.",
          "WAMs add temporal prediction, but usually predict in image or video latent space rather than explicitly geometric space.",
          "GAM predicts future geometry and action tokens inside a GFM, making CAMERA robustness a direct consequence of the policy pathway rather than an auxiliary feature."
        ]
      },
      "id": "60aba31d"
    },
    {
      "type": "abstract",
      "visible": true,
      "sectionLabel": "ABSTRACT",
      "title": "",
      "content": {
        "text": "Generalist robot policies must follow user instructions while reasoning about how objects, cameras, and robot actions interact in the 3D physical world. Recent vision-language-action models and video world-action models inherit strong semantic or temporal priors from large-scale foundation models, but they still operate primarily on 2D image frames or 2D-derived latent spaces, leaving the geometry required for contact-rich manipulation implicit.\n\nWe propose Geometric Action Model (GAM), a language-conditioned manipulation policy that directly repurposes a pretrained Geometric Foundation Model (GFM) as a shared substrate for perception, temporal prediction, and action decoding. GAM splits the GFM at an intermediate layer, where the shallow layers serve as an observation encoder, while a causal future predictor is inserted at the split layer to forecast future latent tokens conditioned on language, proprioception, and action history. The predicted future tokens are then routed through the remaining GFM blocks during feature propagation and decoding, allowing a single backbone to produce both future geometry and actions.\n\nThis design equips the GFM with language-conditioned temporal world modeling using only minimal architectural modification, while preserving its rich geometric priors. On a broad suite of simulation and real-robot manipulation benchmarks, GAM is more accurate, more robust, faster, and lighter than current foundation-model-scale baselines."
      },
      "id": "1357577a"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "visible": true,
      "sectionLabel": "METHOD",
      "title": "Causal Future Predictor Inside the GFM",
      "content": {
        "media": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "",
            "caption": "Observation encoding, future prediction, and action decoding inside one GFM.",
            "alt": "Figure 2 from paper - GAM architecture with GFM shallow layers, language and robot-state conditioning, block-causal future predictor, GFM deep layers, future geometry decoding, and action chunk prediction."
          }
        ]
      },
      "id": "678ebceb"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "visible": true,
      "sectionLabel": "METHOD",
      "title": "Shared Geometry-Action Token Space",
      "content": {
        "text": "GAM splits the pretrained GFM at layer $L_s$ and inserts a causal future predictor between shallow observation encoding and deep geometric decoding.\n\nThe predictor receives language, proprioception, previous action, and geometric tokens, then forecasts both future latent geometry and action tokens. The remaining GFM blocks propagate those predicted tokens, letting the final action head benefit from the same 3D decoding pathway used for future depth.\n\nThe training objective couples action execution with world modeling: $L_{total}=lambda_{act}L_{act}+lambda_{feat}L_{feat}+lambda_{depth}L_{depth}$."
      },
      "id": "832bcbd5"
    },
